Unicorn on a WebAssembly host: TCG's interpreter (TCI) for the translated code.

Unicorn 2 is QEMU 5.0.1's TCG with a code generator for each host CPU it runs on; WebAssembly can
have none (no memory is executable). QEMU's interpreter, which Unicorn 2 left out, runs the
translated code instead: tcg/tci.c and tcg/tci/ as QEMU 5.0.1 has them (GPL-2.0-or-later, Stefan
Weil), on Unicorn's TCG — the register sets per TCGContext, the interpreter one per guest
architecture (UNICORN_ARCH_POSTFIX), as all of TCG is here.

And its helper calls: QEMU 5.0's TCI calls every helper as one type with twelve arguments, which
natively goes unnoticed; a WebAssembly host checks the type of every call through a pointer. The
call now carries the helper's type, from its sizemask (tcg.c, tci_call_sig: each parameter and the
result a 32-bit or 64-bit integer, at most six parameters), and tci_call (tcg/tci-call.inc.c,
written by packages/unicorn/tci-call-gen.py) calls the helper as that type. GETPC() is where the
interpreter is in the translated code (tci_tb_ptr, per thread), as QEMU's TCI has it:
__builtin_return_address is no return address on WebAssembly.

The host: CMakeLists.txt and qemu/configure take __wasm32__ for a host CPU with the interpreter
(CONFIG_TCG_INTERPRETER).

